      "title": "NSE IPO likely to be priced at ₹1,700-1,800, OFS may be trimmed to 5.25%",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/markets/ipo/nse-ipo-price-band-ofs-size-listing-september-2026-126090900832_1.html",
      "description": "The OFS-only issue may be smaller than earlier envisaged as some shareholders prefer to wait for a potentially better price after listing, sources said",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-09T09:28:52"
    },
    {
      "title": "NSE IPO may shrink to ₹25,000-27,000 crore as investors pull back from OFS",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/markets/ipo/nse-ipo-may-shrink-to-25-000-27-000-crore-as-investors-pull-back-from-ofs-126090900665_1.html",
      "description": "The size of the National Stock Exchange (NSE) IPO may shrink with the offer for sale (OFS) likely to be cut to 5.2-5.5 per cent, from the 6 per cent planned earlier, as some shareholders have backed out of the sale, people familiar with the matter said on Wednesday.\nThe reduced OFS could bring down the overall issue size to Rs 25,000-27,000 crore, compared with the earlier estimate of Rs 30,000 crore.At this size, NSE may fall short of becoming India's largest-ever public offering. Hyundai Motor India's IPO, at Rs 27,870 crore, currently holds the record.\nThe reduction in the OFS size is understood to be driven by some shareholders choosing not to sell during the IPO, as they believe they could command a better valuation by selling their stake at a later stage, thepeople familiar with the matter said.\nThe NSE is also looking to make the issue more attractive for retail investors, with pricing being structured to provide greater participation and benefit to small investors in the OFS",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-09T08:41:43"
    },
    {
      "title": "NSE lowers IPO price range, giving up shot at India's top listing",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/markets/ipo/nse-lowers-ipo-price-range-giving-up-shot-at-india-s-top-listing-126090900156_1.html",
      "description": "At the top of the proposed range, the sale of a 5.5% stake would raise about ₹24,300 crore, below the ₹27,900 crore raised by Hyundai Motor India Ltd. in 2024 in the country's largest-ever IPO",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-09T03:30:29"
    }
  ]
